In recent decades, various storage media, such as memory cards, USB sticks or DVDs have been used to back up data.%Aufgabentext

\begin{aufgabenstellung}
\item The storage capacity of a storage medium can be given in kilobytes, megabytes or gigabytes, for example. The prefixes \textit{kilo-, mega-, giga-} are used as follows:

1 megabyte\,$=$\,1\,024 kilobytes\\
1 gigabyte\,$=$\,1\,024 megabytes\vspace{0,3cm}

A particular memory card with a capacity of 16 gigabytes is used to store photos. For the purpose of the model, it is assumed that all of the photos require the same amount of memory.\\
The function $N$: $\mathbb{R}^+\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^+$ assigns the required memory $F$ for one photo to the largest possible number $N(F)$ of photos that can be stored on this memory card ($F$ in kilobytes).%Aufgabentext

\Subitem{Write down an equation of the function $N$.\vspace{0,3cm}

$N(F)=$\,\antwort[\rule{5cm}{0.3pt}]{$\dfrac{16\,777\,216}{F}$}} %Unterpunkt1
%\Subitem{•} %Unterpunkt2

\item Michael has 4 USB sticks labelled $A, B, C$ and $D$.
\begin{itemize}
\item On USB stick $A$, he saves all of his photos.
\item On the 3 other USB sticks, $B$, $C$ and $D$, he saves exactly one third of all of his photos so that every photo is also stored on exactly 1 of these 3 USB sticks as a backup.
\end{itemize}

For each of the 4 USB sticks, the probability that it still works after 5 years is 75\,\% (independent of the other sticks).

As a simplification, it can be assumed that a USB stick either works fully or does not work at all.

\Subitem{Determine the probability that after 5 years every one of Michael’s photos is still available on at least 1 USB stick.} %Unterpunkt1

After 5 years, Michael realises that USB stick A no longer works.

\Subitem{Determine the probability that at least 2 of the 3 USB sticks $B$, $C$ and $D$ still work.} %Unterpunkt2

\item A popular storage medium for films is the DVD.

Since the beginning of the 21st century, the average price of a film DVD has decreased, as shown in the diagram below.

