Container ships make low-cost transport of large quantities of a wide variety of goods at sea possible.

In shipping, distances are given in nautical miles (NM), and speeds are given in knots (kn).%Aufgabentext

\begin{aufgabenstellung}
\item The course of a container ship is modelled by the velocity vectors $\vec{k}$, $\vec{a}$ and $\vec{s}$.

The target course $\vec{k}$ is the result of the intended course $\vec{s}$ and the so-called drift $\vec{a}$.

$\vec{k} = \vec{s} + \vec{a}$

For a particular point in time, the velocity vector $\vec{k}$ and $\vec{a}$ are shown in the diagram below.

\begin{center}
