\item Ms Egger receives a dose of active ingredient B at time $t=0$.\\
The real function $m_B$ with $m_B(t)=1{.}019\cdot t\cdot e{-0{.}025\cdot t}$ models the amount of active ingredient B in Ms Egger’s body in terms of the time ($t$ in min, $m_B(t)$ in mg).

From the time $t=240$\,min, the amount of active ingredient B in Ms Egger’s body can be modelled by the linear function $f$ ($t$ in min, $f(t)$ in mg).

The functions $m_B$ and $f$ have the same value and the same gradient when $t=240$\,min.

 %Aufgabentext

\Subitem{Write down an equation of the function $f$.\vspace{0,5cm}

$f(t)=\,\antwort[\rule{3cm}{0.3pt}]{-0,0126\ldots\cdot t+3,6372\ldots}$} %Unterpunkt1	

\item The probability of experiencing nausea as a side effect after taking active ingredient C is 14\,\%.

$n$ people who have taken active ingredient C are selected at random. The number of people who experience nausea as a side effect is assumed to be binomially distributed.%Aufgabentext

