\end{center}

Directly before the jump, the jumper is at point $J=(0{.}5, 8)$ and the blobber is at point $B=(8, 2{.}4)$.  The jumper sees the blobber through the angle of depression $\alpha$.%Aufgabentext

\Subitem{Determine $\alpha$.} %Unterpunkt1

The function $h$ with $h(x)=a\cdot x^2+b\cdot x+c$ with $a,b,c\in\mathbb{R}$ and $a\neq 0$ odels the flight path of the blobber from the launch point to the point, where the blobber lands on the surface of the water.

$x\ldots$ horizontal distance of the blobber from the diving platform in m\\
$h(x)\ldots$ height of the blobber above the surface of the water at $x$ in m

The maximum height of the blobber corresponds to 65\,\% of the height of the jumping off point and is reached at a horizontal distance of 10.5\,m from the diving platform.

\Subitem{Write down a system of equations that can be used to calculate the coefficients $a$, $b$ and $c$ of the function $h$. } %Unterpunkt2

According to this model, the blobber lands on the surface of the water at a particular horizontal distance from the diving platform.

\Subitem{Determine this distance.}

\item In a particular blobbing competition, the heights reached by 20 blobbers are measured to the nearest decimetre.

The following results are published:

\begin{itemize}
\item The lowest height recorded is 1.2\,m, and the greatest height recorded is 7.9\,m.
\item Exactly 7 blobbers reached a height of at most 3\,m.
\item Exactly 10 blobbers exceeded a height of 3\,m and reached a height of at most 4.5\,m.
